# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# ROUND 4, CYCLE 2 on coolify, SEVEN-STEP WORK SESSION
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/03-expand.md). SEVEN steps, names verbatim and in the
# order of scenarios/coolify.yaml - the judge pairs by name and nothing else.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS. The rubric of the superseded seven-step
# (scenarios/coolify.rubric.yaml.session-baseline-assembled.bak), carried forward with exactly
# ONE block rewritten: `Orient in the codebase`. Every other block, including the discriminator,
# is unchanged.
#
# WHY THAT ONE BLOCK MOVED. The superseded scenario asked for the affected population TWICE -
# its step 1 asked "which parts of that layout is the subject touched from at all? Name each
# part once, with a single example located at file:line", and its step 3 asked for "the areas
# it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line". Its paid-gate
# validation returned DO-NOT-PAY: baseline 7 of 13 = 0.538 against sense 8 of 13 = 0.615,
# delta +0.077, with results/loop/coolify/validate.md reading the route as "given three prior
# steps of context, the baseline's own earlier greps had already surfaced the periphery, so
# step 4 became a re-read rather than a discovery". The scenario now spends that sweep once,
# at the end of step 3. This rubric follows: step 1 scores a map of the PRODUCT, and scoring
# "which parts the subject is reached from" is now explicitly withheld there, because rewarding
# it is how a rubric buys back an enumeration the ask just refused.
#
# LEFT ALONE, DELIBERATELY: the block for `Audit every dependent of the server contract` is the
# probe rubric's step-2 block, sliced out verbatim rather than retyped. That step is the
# discriminator; its prompt and its thirteen scored rows are byte-identical to what this cycle's
# mini-bench measured (baseline 1 of 13 = 0.077, sense 11 of 13 = 0.846, delta +0.769), and
# re-writing how it is JUDGED would move the number as surely as re-writing the ask.
#
# WHAT CARRIES FORWARD FROM THE PROBE RUBRIC AND MUST KEEP CARRYING.
#   1. NO STEP BEFORE THE DISCRIMINATOR SCORES AN ENUMERATION. An earlier attempt's step 1
#      asked for the whole working ground one located line at a time; its baseline wrote a
#      covering token sweep to satisfy that and re-read the same output in the next step,
#      closing at 11 of 14 dependents. So `Orient in the codebase` scores a MAP and explicitly
#      not a roll, and the population part of `Trace the write path and the teardown` scores
#      the SHAPE - areas, one located example each, and reach - with an exhaustive list earning
#      nothing extra there.
#   2. THE DISCRIMINATOR SCORES WHERE THE PLACE LIVES, not only what it keeps: measured across
#      every run on the headline model, the baseline sits at 0.70 on rows in machine-named paths
#      against 0.35 on rows outside them, and all thirteen scored paths are outside them.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight on the discriminator: the answer there is not a set
# of locations, it is a set of KEPT PIECES WITH LIFETIMES. Two lines of nearly identical text
# differ entirely in how long the value they keep outlives the machine - one request, one stored
# row, one cache entry, or another system altogether - and therefore in what can still be
# corrected when identity is reworked. Naming that is the answer.
#
# WHY specificity demands the routine AND the lifetime AND the failure: a file:line here is not
# self-explaining, and the failure this criterion exists to catch is a run of places named
# together under one shared reason with no location on any of them.
#
# THE STEPS AFTER THE DISCRIMINATOR ARE JUDGED ON WHAT THEY ADD, NOT ON RESTATEMENT. The guards
# step is scored on the ABSENCES it names as much as the guards it finds; the blast-radius step
# on whether the order is driven by consequence and by whether a failure is silent, not by
# confidence; the plan on whether a teammate could execute it without re-deriving anything, and
# on whether "needs no change" is kept distinct from "needs re-reading to be sure".
audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely rework what identifies a remote machine
  in Coolify - the record, its number, its handle, its display name and its address - given that
  after the rework a machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a
  different one. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things: how a machine's
  identity works today - where the identifying fields are set as the record is written, where a
  lookup by identity is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store and where that
  cache is emptied, where a freshly created machine seeds further records including any that
  take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it, and what the removal path
  does and does not clean up - each traced through the routines that make it up and each
  located; and how far the change reaches BEYOND the code that is handed a machine and works
  with it in hand.
  That far layer is what decides the answer. It is every place that took one identifying piece
  off a machine, kept it after the machine itself was gone from its hands, and later trusts it:
  a number stored on a row that belongs to something else entirely; a number written onto a
  queued or historical record and read back much later to act on the machine it names; a handle
  concatenated into a cache key or a queue lock name that survives a restart; a number kept in a
  screen's state between requests and resolved again on the next one; a number written into a
  pivot row as a column of the join itself; a handle written into a message body that leaves
  this product for a system we do not control; a bare number arriving back on an event and
  resolved as though it still meant the same machine; a helper whose whole contract with its
  callers is a number rather than the machine. Each must be pinned to an exact file:line, with
  the routine named, with how long the piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one
  request, inside one stored record, or outside this product entirely - and with what that place
  starts getting wrong the first time the piece no longer resolves.
  Almost all of that layer sits in code whose own job is something else: notifications about
  containers, a search box, a creation wizard, a log viewer, a cleanup history record, a shared
  helper. An answer that stays in the part of the product that is obviously about machines has
  found the ground, not the answer.
  A weak answer stops at the code that is handed a machine, however densely it lists it; or
  collapses a run of places into one line with a shared reason and no location on any of them;
  or gives file:line without the lifetime and the failure, which leaves the agent knowing where
  a value is written and not what breaks when it stops meaning what it meant.
steps:
- name: Orient in the codebase
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Two things, and the first is a gate on the rest of the session: is the ONE type that
        stands for one of these machines named, and located at the file:line where it is
        defined, and treated from there on as a single subject rather than as a word to search
        for? An answer that leaves the subject as a token, or that names several types as
        candidates without settling on one, has failed the part everything later is built on.
        Then the layout, and score it as a map of the PRODUCT: does the answer describe how this
        codebase is organised in the tree's own terms rather than in handed-down categories, and
        does each part it names carry a phrase on the kind of work that part does? Score whether
        the parts are genuinely distinct and correctly characterised.
        Withhold credit for two things here, both of them deliberate. An enumeration of any
        part's contents: it was not asked for, and a long located list is a worse answer than a
        short accurate map, because the budget it spends is taken from the later parts. And a
        roll of where the subject is reached from: the step forbids it in as many words, that
        question is asked once later and in a particular form, and an answer that supplies it
        here has done the session's central sweep twice out of one budget. Neither absence is a
        gap - do not mark the answer down for either.
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Is the subject's definition given as an exact file:line, not a filename and not a class
        name alone? Does every part of the layout carry one concrete example located at
        file:line, rather than a directory named in the abstract? A part asserted with no
        located example is not specific.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part of the layout come with a phrase on the kind of work it does - what that
        part is FOR - rather than a restatement of the path? Is the claim that this is the only
        type standing for one of these machines actually justified, or merely asserted?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it opened and read from what it is taking on the strength
        of a name or a familiar framework convention, and name what it could not settle? At this
        point in the session almost nothing has been read, so an orientation offered as
        established fact is worth less than one that says which parts are still assumptions.
- name: Map the server contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Two halves, and they are NOT weighted alike, because the step bounds the first and
        leans on the second.
        The composition: is what the type is BUILT OUT OF laid out - the shared behaviour it
        mixes in and the other records it declares a relationship to - and, crucially, are the
        things it pulls in for its own use separated from the things that hang off it and exist
        only because it does? That direction is what makes the later steps tractable and
        getting it backwards is a real error, not a presentation choice. It is asked for as a
        located list and NOT as an essay: a phrase on each declaration earns nothing here, and
        an extended commentary on the composition is a worse answer than a correct list,
        because the budget it spends is taken from the discriminator.
        The identity, which is the half that carries: are the identifying fields named and
        located, and is the per-request cache that serves lookups by identity found, together
        with what empties it and when? The question is coverage of those two jobs and whether
        they are connected into one story about how identity is established and resolved,
        rather than listed as places a name is mentioned. Could the next steps act from this
        map with targeted lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every declaration, field and routine carry an exact file:line AND, where it is a
        routine, the name it is defined under? A bare filename is not specific. A relationship
        or a mixed-in behaviour asserted from the shape of the class, without the line where it
        is declared, is not specific. Where the answer claims a lookup is served from a
        per-request cache, is the line that does the caching located, and the line that empties
        it, rather than inferred from a name?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does the identity half carry a phrase on what each part contributes - what it sets,
        what it caches and on what key, what empties it? Does the closing sentence say what a
        rework of identity would have to PRESERVE for this contract to keep meaning what it
        means today, rather than restating the names of the parts? Do NOT penalise a
        composition list offered without commentary: the step asks for a phrase there only
        where the direction is not obvious from the declaration.
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Relation accessors, model event hooks and cached lookups invite confident guessing from
        framework convention alone. Does the answer say which parts it actually opened, and name
        what it could not resolve rather than smoothing it over? Is the composition offered as
        what it established rather than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Trace the write path and the teardown
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Three things, and the third is the one the rest of the session runs on.
        CREATION: is what runs when one of these machines is first written traced IN ORDER, with
        everything seeded from it named - and is each seeded thing ruled explicitly as holding a
        LINK back to the machine or a COPY of one of the identifying fields? That ruling is the
        step's centre of gravity: an answer that lists the seeded records without making the
        call on each one has described the path and missed the point of tracing it.
        TEARDOWN: is the removal path traced, with what it takes with it, what it hands to
        something else, and - specifically - what it LEAVES BEHIND? "The path looked complete"
        is the failure this part exists to catch.
        THE HAND-OFFS: for the parts of either path that run later and out of process, is it
        said - in one line each, which is all the step asks for - whether the machine record
        itself travels or only something taken off it? That single distinction is the whole of
        this part; an expanded treatment of the queueing machinery is not a better answer, it
        is budget taken from the discriminator.
        Finally the population: is the whole affected set established in ONE sweep to its full
        depth and reported as its SHAPE - the areas it lands in, one located example each, how
        far out from the type it reaches - with the near edge distinguished from the far side
        that is reached only through it? An enumeration of the places themselves earns nothing
        here; what is scored is whether the set was established as one thing, in a form the next
        step can be worked straight down, and whether the far side is present in it at all. An
        answer whose population stops at the near edge has failed this part however well located
        it is.
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every step of the creation path, every seeded thing, every part of the removal path
        and every hand-off carry an exact file:line and the routine it sits in? Is the line that
        actually writes, seeds, copies, deletes or dispatches located, rather than asserted from
        the name of a relation or a class? Does each area of the population carry one concrete
        located example rather than a directory named in the abstract?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each seeded thing carry the reason for its link-or-copy ruling, each hand-off a
        phrase on why it runs out of process and what that means for what travels with it, and
        each thing left behind by the removal path a phrase on what it becomes once the machine
        is gone? Is the reach claim on the population argued - why the far side is further out
        than one step - rather than asserted?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Model event hooks, queued dispatches and cascading deletes are exactly where framework
        convention invites assertion without reading. Does the answer say which of these it
        opened? Where it cannot tell whether something is cleaned up, or whether a hand-off
        carries the record or a piece of it, does it say so and say what would settle it? Is the
        population offered as the sweep it ran, with its depth stated, rather than as a claim of
        completeness it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it has three parts: THE KEPT PIECE, WHERE THE PLACE LIVES, and
        BREADTH OF AREA. The working ground - code handed one of these machines that acts on it
        in hand - is explicitly out, so a near-edge list earns nothing here however dense or well
        located, and restating the previous step's orientation is not progress. What is asked for
        is the line where a piece of a machine is lifted off it and put somewhere the machine
        itself cannot be carried to, or read back out of somewhere after the machine is gone.
        Does the audit reach that layer - a number stored on a row whose subject is something
        else entirely, orphaned rather than repointed when the machine goes; a number written
        onto a queued or historical record and read back much later to act on the machine it
        names; a number written into a pivot row as a column of the join; a handle concatenated
        into a cache key or a lock name that survives the request and the process; a number kept
        in a screen's state across the several requests of a flow and resolved again each time;
        a handle written into a message body that leaves this product entirely; a bare number
        arriving back on an event and resolved as though it still meant the same machine; a
        helper or a job that matches its own machine against numbers stored by an earlier
        process?
        WHERE THE PLACE LIVES is scored, not just what it keeps. This layer sits overwhelmingly
        in code whose own job is something else - a container notification, a search box, a
        creation or clone wizard, a log viewer, a cleanup history row, a shared helper, a queued
        job about containers - and hardly at all in the part of the product that is obviously
        about machines, which is where the ground lives. An answer concentrated in the
        machine-named tree and thin everywhere else has enumerated inside one search scope and
        mistaken that for the codebase; score it as the partial answer it is, however well
        located each line.
        Above all: does the answer state, for each place, HOW LONG THE PIECE OUTLIVES THE MACHINE
        - one request, one stored record, or outside this product - which is the distinction a
        reading of the line alone does not give, and the distinction that decides what can still
        be corrected? Where the answer ran short, is what it had still not looked at declared
        rather than quietly closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does EVERY place carry its own exact file:line AND the name of the routine the line sits
        inside AND the lifetime of the piece it keeps? All three, because a location alone does
        not identify this kind of dependency: two lines of nearly identical shape differ entirely
        depending on whether the value dies with the request, sits in a stored row, or has
        already left this product. Several places named together on one line as a set that shares
        a reason is the failure this criterion exists to catch: however correct the shared reason
        is, it is one item, not a set of located places, and it should be scored as one. A bare
        filename does not count.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each place carry one phrase on what IT would start getting wrong the first time the
        piece it kept no longer resolves to the machine it came from - pointing at the wrong
        machine, at no machine, reusing a connection that is not there, taking a listener down on
        a failed lookup, treating a live deployment as inactive, cancelling on the wrong host,
        orphaning a row, authorising against a number rather than an object - rather than a
        generic restatement that identity is used here? Is the consequence specific to that
        place, or the same sentence copied down the list?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Where the answer could not tell whether a value is a kept piece or a machine still in
        hand, does it say so and say what it would take to settle it, rather than asserting?
        Is the coverage claim honest - what areas were swept, which were not reached, and whether
        the list is offered as complete or as what this session got to? A confident "that is all
        of them" with an unexamined area behind it is worth less than a shorter list that names
        the gap.
- name: Trace the guards
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        The step asks how the product satisfies itself that it is acting on a real, CURRENT
        machine before it acts, and it asks for three shapes plus one absence - explicitly as
        worked examples and NOT as a census.
        Does the answer distinguish lookups that RAISE on failure from lookups that quietly hand
        back nothing - and, in the quiet case, say what the calling code then does having been
        handed nothing, which is the part that decides whether the failure is loud or silent?
        Does it find where a lookup is narrowed to what the caller is entitled to see and where
        it is not narrowed at all? Does it find where two identifying pieces are compared as
        plain values, with no machine present on either side, to decide that two things are the
        same machine?
        Above all: is at least one same-shaped line that carries NO guard named? The absence is
        the finding in this step, and an answer listing only the guarded cases has inverted it -
        score it as the partial answer it is however well located each guard is. Restating the
        earlier steps' lines with the word "guard" attached is not progress.
        Score COVERAGE OF THE SHAPES, not the count of instances: one correct located example
        of each shape is a complete answer here, and an exhaustive sweep of every guard in the
        product earns nothing extra, because the budget it spends was taken from elsewhere in
        the session.
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every guard, and every named absence, carry its own exact file:line AND the routine
        the line sits inside? Is the line that performs the check located, rather than the method
        that contains it named in general terms? Several guards collapsed onto one line as a set
        sharing a reason is one item, not a set, however correct the shared reason is.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each guard carry one phrase on what it ACTUALLY protects against - a machine that is
        gone, a machine that is someone else's, a machine that was replaced, a state that has
        moved on - specific to that line rather than a generic statement that the code checks
        something? Where a guard is deliberately not narrowed, is the reason given rather than
        the omission reported as a defect?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate the guards it read from the ones it is asserting from the shape
        of a call, and say what it could not settle? Is the sweep offered as what this session
        reached, with the parts it did not reach named, rather than as the complete set of guards
        in the product?
- name: Assess the blast radius
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is there an actual ORDER, driven by consequence, that a maintainer could work straight
        down - or a re-listing of the places with severity words attached? The step is explicit
        that the rank is by what happens when the piece stops resolving and NOT by how likely
        the answer thinks that is, so a ranking driven by the answer's own confidence has
        answered a different question.
        Is each place AT THE TOP OF THE ORDER classified by how the failure would SURFACE - a
        lookup that raises and takes something down, a silent nothing the code carries on with,
        a different machine acted on with no complaint at all, or a wrong value that has already
        left this product and cannot be corrected from here - and does the order reflect that
        the silent ones outrank the loud ones, with the reason given once?
        The step asks for the top of the order in full and for the tail in a line, so a
        re-listing of every place the audit already gave is NOT a better answer here: it is a
        restatement, and the step says so. What is scored is whether the top was chosen by
        consequence and whether the tail is accounted for rather than silently dropped.
        Then: is what must be re-verified after the rework named for those top places, and is
        each re-verification placed somewhere concrete rather than left as an instruction to
        test carefully? A missed high-risk dependent is the one that pages someone, so an order
        whose top is wrong is worse than one whose tail is short.
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place in the top of the order carry its own exact file:line, its rank, and
        its surfacing class? Is each re-verification located at a file:line where it would
        actually be done? Places grouped into a band with one shared justification and no
        location on any of them is the failure this criterion exists to catch - it is one item,
        not a band of ranked places. The single line accounting for the tail is exempt: it is
        what the step asked for there.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each rank carry one sentence saying what goes wrong, to whom or to what, and whether
        anything catches it - specific to that place rather than the same sentence copied down
        the order? Is the claim that the silent failures outrank the loud ones argued rather than
        stated?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Where the answer is ranking something it did not open, does it say so rather than
        smoothing it into the order? Is the order offered as a judgement with its basis stated,
        with the places it could not rank named, rather than as a settled severity table?
- name: Plan the change and the verification map
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Could a teammate execute this plan without re-deriving anything? Three parts.
        THE EDIT: are the lines the answer would change in the contract, its write path and its
        removal path given, in the order they would be changed, with what each change is?
        DOWNSTREAM: is what has to move with it grouped by area, with one line per area saying
        what changes there and one located example - and are the places whose change is NOT the
        same as the rest of their area's then named SEPARATELY at file:line? That second part is
        what makes the plan executable, because those are the only ones a teammate cannot infer
        from the area line. Is "needs no change" kept distinct from "needs no change but must be
        re-read to be sure"? Those are two different claims and collapsing them is the failure
        this part names. A full re-listing of the audit is NOT a stronger answer here - the step
        rules it out, and it is budget taken from the tests below.
        THE TESTS: are the tests that already assert something about how a machine is identified,
        resolved, seeded or torn down named, each at the assertion or setup line that would have
        to change, with what changes about it - and where nothing existing covers a place the
        previous step ranked high, is that gap stated plainly and the missing test described,
        rather than a plausible-looking test file named as though it existed? Naming a file that
        does not exist is worse than declaring the gap.
        Finally, is there an order of operations that keeps the product working while the rework
        lands, and is what cannot be split from what identified?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every edit, every separately-named downstream place and every existing test carry
        its own exact file:line - for the tests, the line of the assertion or the setup that
        changes, not the test file alone? Does each downstream AREA carry at least its one
        located example? A bare filename does not count anywhere in this step, and a described
        future test is scored on the description, not on a location it cannot have.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each change carry a phrase on why it is needed and what it makes safe? Does each
        "must be re-read" carry the doubt that makes it worth re-reading? Does the order of
        operations argue why that order keeps the product working, rather than presenting a list?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Is the plan honest about its own coverage - which areas it walked, which it did not
        reach, and whether the downstream list is offered as complete or as what this session
        got to? Are the gaps in test coverage named as gaps rather than filled with a
        confident-sounding file? A shorter plan that names what it did not settle is worth more
        than a complete-looking one with an unexamined area behind it.
